  "Number": 10,
  "Question": "Let \\(r\\) be the number that results when both the base and the exponent of \\(a^b\\) are tripled, where \\(a,\\) \\(b\\gt0.\\) If \\(r\\) equals the product of \\(a^b\\) and \\(x^b\\) where \\(x\\gt0,\\) then \\(x=\\)",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(3\\)",
    "B": "\\(3a^2\\)",
    "C": "\\(27a^2\\)",
    "D": "\\(2a^{3b}\\)",
    "E": "\\(3a^{2b}\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "C",
  "Solution": "Tripling the base and exponent gives \\(r=(3a)^{3b}\\). To compare this with \\(a^b x^b\\), write both sides as powers with exponent \\(b\\):\n\\[\n\\bigl((3a)^3\\bigr)^b=(ax)^b.\n\\]\nAll bases are positive, and \\(b>0\\), so taking the \\(1/b\\) power of both sides is valid. The power-of-a-power rule gives\n\\[\n(3a)^3=ax,\n\\qquad 27a^3=ax.\n\\]\nSince \\(a>0\\), divide by \\(a\\) to obtain \\(\\boxed{x=27a^2}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{C}}\\). These steps are reversible for positive \\(x\\), so this is the unique allowed value.",
